Q: Why doesn’t JavaScript provide a native way to cancel Promise.all when one promise fails? A: <p>You can’t cancel promises directly, but you can cancel the underlying async operations if they support it (e.g. <code>fetch</code> via <code>AbortController</code>).</p>
<p>The key is to make all requests share the same <code>AbortSignal</code>, so you can cancel them together.</p>
<pre><code>const controller = new AbortController();
const { signal } = controller;

const promises = [
  fetch(&quot;/api/1&quot;, { signal }),
  fetch(&quot;/api/2&quot;, { signal }),
  fetch(&quot;/api/3&quot;, { signal }),
];

try {
  await Promise.all(promises);
} catch (err) {
  controller.abort(); // cancel remaining requests
  throw err;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Custom async code doesn't support AbortController and must be implemented manually.</p>
